{:edb/db-before-t 1353 :edb/db-after-t 1354 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534666 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:38:39.887Z" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133026 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133030 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133587 1071 "For the vector ![](images/b8/b85705767814eae0f99345888b1f0c65a89dbbc76458d7093a02a20937d2e1c3.png) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/85/85cd8d0ebcd70de93ec2bd5007907a02d81daadd27b348e7c72ccef53654d881.png)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/e0/e03fd2577b8d7067f4507d0ee6fe732d3786a494134cd967d3c65de01e28e709.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133587 1071 "For the vector ![](images/c3/c32c8627f43b93123b110207a136416c9b498a9b0fbab9adc671e039839f098c.svg) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/62/621d5f7a19f100f4c7089900b4ef61851bb23ad580127a03c0050bdb295e1802.svg)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/5e/5e3fe028385135bd1e744ae7ee0e0d6ed319bf5fc145ae70bee0b30991008964.svg)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133588 1071 "Given that ![](images/60/6052e4c3f0ad4e3ddf02b9200c89e808b24a8820de6445677ef0d685927b7b80.svg) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](images/c0/c09dc7dd7a9e4b4493e3c1e2c1324dffe991e8948a371c0ab9d4de9828c280a3.svg) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133588 1071 "Given that ![](images/d6/d648a1f36d090c680a6b72f30ff447ce78623f746157348f30a02fa4ea245c66.png) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](images/d2/d29f02d59f540cae1aae6057e05eeeb349d84de1ac9be11f6e3b131083f6905a.png) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133588 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](images/60/608fd40128068aa5f05e6b256081bd2e0ebbf569fca4c9bae4977740eb4db2ce.png)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/d6/d6d7ea3c500d1112c64e19da288fbd7ad7e6ed912cf3121a2292427368b19f0e.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](images/c4/c4875bc998184d934c9b43113cd31091b792f1c04ff4387d4b8ab8a7a08f90d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133588 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](images/fa/fafbc63ea1e75cd6d7f1dfc93e7e5a83d8f570c6c3a7b356f818debbb091cdfb.svg)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/a9/a9bbd5f866831a70146beb08aa61180fe7dc3aa27e10a767e5fe070de675349e.svg)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](images/32/324a22d18daa5c69f63e89036b6e728a41b8d2eb5a0b13422d733b8625ec1652.svg)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133589 1071 "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](images/55/5511eb6c1d36976fdf7e1e9a5fdaeef489d425b227dcd0a4cbf857933c740ed0.svg)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133589 1071 "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](images/88/889411800149371acab53c44829a050d69df697ce6723dd95b9045ddf873e484.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133589 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](images/98/986f6778009507b2fd597e22ce9427e1220932d547b5e16fa664309ab6e719ce.svg) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](images/ae/aea42e47226db466060875ea1ac0f4603c45194bbad87d431f9b5fbacaccf51a.svg) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](images/e5/e5f6a6c2b497838b064a43264e85a031ad431633228401507a599167cc970c5c.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](images/6e/6e3de0d6e9be6bc505c6aa37ab412a6a51915e08cf7bf0d34a33ec79aa6d9181.svg)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133589 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](images/ed/ed0e0a3a78ff25be588f62627ffd5bae4074f6c59b8e88ad160e4407253ae6dd.png) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](images/af/af507041d4aa3cc7dfb415afc672b5a7875f9f1a27acced81505797e5f92d7e0.png) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](images/34/34b29c1902a7c441b2ac15be91ffb60b8856ab7dc2706146d09eb02f081827ba.png)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](images/49/496154a4d67c0b7861da038306f1d5cc65188cc036cf8514b24603c7ce4cd948.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133632 1071 "Consider the vector subspace ![](images/20/208ca9a2a786c6c059c9bad9a129fe386c79e48e422b5dfa7278dd74c785067e.svg) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](images/8e/8e7da4f04ac9eae56aa22a780ee5130e9b3dcf5f8adbcae139e7a415fd54ac83.svg)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133632 1071 "Consider the vector subspace ![](images/be/bee23fc9beaddffe7fd60b2c2bbd2d2ee952a277960c5d08fcad5b934e209f64.png) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](images/ab/abfbcbc8c3b942b93faf4b48336daa54345b97fb21d3aa11685f0d29f206590e.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186133778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431859 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431860 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431858 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186133989 1050 1080.0 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186134227 1071 "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186134227 1071 "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186134227 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186134227 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186134498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186134498 1071 "Given that $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$, find the value of $\\frac{b + c}{a}$." 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186134498 1071 "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186134498 1074 "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=20,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{20+4}{3}=8.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186135043 1064 "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors ![](images/b9/b986146bf3d9edbf85c9047aa0e0acac54b9dd962c5c1f1b2d1612a36444337b.svg) Can we find a *non-zero* vector ![](images/2b/2be010cc1a39d762cb78272a1c6f8b10574823fa549ba2e54066f15c1d312ff9.svg) that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n![](images/4a/4aee36f32445ae5fe5286d7a5aa7600f1f7fc8449fb981509cbd999822408c90.svg)\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186135043 1064 "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ Can we find a *non-zero* vector $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" 13194139534666 17592186045417 false] [17592186135044 1064 "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\n  \n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector in $H.$\n\nTo check whether a vector belongs to $H^{\\perp},$ we use the following theorem:\n\n*A vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if it is orthogonal to a set of vectors that spans $H$. *\n\nIn other words:\n\n*If $H=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\},$ then $\\mathbf{v} \\in H^{\\perp}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\}.$*\n\nFor example, if the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2,$ then the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of $H$ consists of all vectors that are perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8cf96c9871966fb0a7a921b70229e4a17bb9f6dad1756986f0500c70be6ab95.png)\n\n  \n\nSome other important things to notice about $H^{\\perp}$ are as follows:\n\n- $H^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\n- $(H^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = H$\n\n- $H \\cap H^{\\perp} = \\{\\mathbf 0\\}$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186135044 1064 "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector 