7592186045493 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431856 1077 "selection" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431856 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431854 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431856 1079 #uuid "7977ec2e-bf6e-54d9-95ee-2c321a3e5408" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431837 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431838 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431839 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431843 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431844 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1077 "selection" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431839 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431857 1079 #uuid "a22e3410-aef3-5a94-97ba-9cdaa238fcc3" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431856 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1069 "q-39951" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1072 #uuid "4c6cb332-318e-5ab0-89ce-ace167bccb04" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431858 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot (3)+3\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 4=-17\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-3)=-2 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 4=-31\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\"" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431857 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1069 "q-140464" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1071 "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1072 #uuid "adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431859 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-5)=20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 10+1\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0=15\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431855 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1069 "q-39634" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1071 "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1072 #uuid "fc37a677-a8ea-5147-882f-3adaa2a20db5" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true] [17592186431860 1074 "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3}$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form   \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" 13194139534666 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-059410e3-40e1-5548-b990-cb95702ffd3d" 17592186431835 "answer-1879dc64-b7c0-542f-9bb2-e3524feeea44" 17592186431836 "answer-1e2a07a5-dbe2-541d-8ce4-ee6e5f93fe79" 17592186431837 "answer-35a22425-0fd6-5e91-8dbd-62be5dc9184d" 17592186431838 "answer-3e29f639-6dea-54ac-bb7f-18bb3d312722" 17592186431839 "answer-4ba56a09-8a82-5e35-8607-9429ad0df8ec" 17592186431840 "answer-555c54d9-1d37-5e6e-b4d1-bbbcb88d3fd6" 17592186431841 "answer-5b0019d1-7d0c-5a56-8b1c-2f7a6788a5d2" 17592186431842 "answer-5bfd9fd9-87e6-5685-a09c-de3d488b9e58" 17592186431843 "answer-81f8e02c-48df-5958-a046-15ad41372d47" 17592186431844 "answer-9c557d7d-9732-552d-9575-c8050c68bf6e" 17592186431845 "answer-a4541d70-89bc-50aa-b47d-4de40d32cc70" 17592186431846 "answer-a616221f-a3ff-597c-a09b-75e9b404d12d" 17592186431847 "answer-b92e104b-6b55-5959-8c95-c1a99a06687e" 17592186431848 "answer-c74f6ea3-5aeb-5209-af1b-0ceb2195f015" 17592186431849 "answer-d76919dc-09b9-5af9-ab5a-dfa77d8e2c19" 17592186431850 "answer-e4290193-3591-5159-80ee-76f9d3d6cd1f" 17592186431851 "answer-e7